1
00:00:12,300 --> 00:00:15,120
Reach across time and see yourself.

2
00:00:16,040 --> 00:00:18,580
You stand tall against terrifying enemies.

3
00:00:20,000 --> 00:00:24,620
You build masterpieces and break down barriers.

4
00:00:25,560 --> 00:00:29,140
You rush into the future and meet your past.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:36,040
For every time, at every moment, the official network of every millennium.

6
00:00:37,120 --> 00:00:41,100
The History Channel, where the past comes alive.

7
00:00:42,080 --> 00:00:45,440
Niagara Falls, beauty and raw power.

8
00:00:46,200 --> 00:00:48,520
Mother Nature's brute force on display.

9
00:00:49,580 --> 00:00:54,360
Since the 16th century, some of our greatest minds attempted to harness it.

10
00:00:55,060 --> 00:00:57,320
Finally, spectacular success.

11
00:00:57,321 --> 00:01:01,320
Now, Niagara Power on Modern Marvels.

12
00:01:25,203 --> 00:01:28,520
The volume of water held by one million bathtubs

13
00:01:28,521 --> 00:01:33,520
goes over Niagara Falls' 160-foot drop every second.

14
00:01:33,620 --> 00:01:35,960
Two-thirds of North America's fresh water

15
00:01:36,060 --> 00:01:39,400
passes through its American and Canadian falls.

16
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:42,960
The Niagara River in upstate New York feeds Niagara Falls,

17
00:01:43,060 --> 00:01:46,180
which is located between Lake Erie and Lake Ontario.

18
00:01:49,480 --> 00:01:52,840
Today, the Niagara Power Project that harnesses just

19
00:01:52,940 --> 00:01:56,280
one-third of the falls' hydroelectric potential

20
00:01:56,281 --> 00:02:00,320
is the fifth largest electricity producer in the United States,

21
00:02:00,420 --> 00:02:02,760
as well as the largest electricity producer

22
00:02:02,860 --> 00:02:04,960
in New York State.

23
00:02:05,060 --> 00:02:08,360
This plant, when it was built, was the largest

24
00:02:08,460 --> 00:02:11,660
in the free world.

25
00:02:11,760 --> 00:02:14,240
Consisting of the Robert Moses Power Plant

26
00:02:14,340 --> 00:02:16,160
and Lewiston Pump Generating Plant,

27
00:02:16,260 --> 00:02:19,540
the Niagara Power Project generates enough electricity

28
00:02:19,640 --> 00:02:25,440
to continuously light 24 million 100 watt light bulbs.

29
00:02:25,441 --> 00:02:30,720
It was truly a magnificent feat to complete this facility in a little over three years.

30
00:02:32,720 --> 00:02:40,200
Houses had to be relocated, bridges had to be built, highways had to be relocated, as well as railroad tracks.

31
00:02:41,320 --> 00:02:48,160
The amount of excavation that went on, I believe it's something on the order of 25 million cubic yards.

32
00:02:48,540 --> 00:02:50,560
I mean, just the numbers are staggering.

33
00:02:50,561 --> 00:02:55,360
While the Niagara Power Project utilizes state-of-the-art

34
00:02:55,361 --> 00:02:59,800
technology, the idea of harnessing the power of Niagara Falls

35
00:02:59,801 --> 00:03:02,800
and the Niagara River was almost always ahead

36
00:03:02,801 --> 00:03:04,800
of the existing technology.

37
00:03:07,680 --> 00:03:10,680
Niagara Falls was formed nearly 12,000 years ago

38
00:03:10,681 --> 00:03:13,680
when the North American ice sheet started to recede

39
00:03:13,681 --> 00:03:17,680
and formed one giant freshwater lake called Lundy,

40
00:03:17,681 --> 00:03:22,680
Lake Lundy, which now consists of four of the Great Lakes.

41
00:03:23,680 --> 00:03:26,680
The runoff from Lake Lundy formed the basin of a lower lake,

42
00:03:26,681 --> 00:03:30,680
which is now known as Lake Ontario, the fifth of the Great Lakes.

43
00:03:30,681 --> 00:03:34,680
As vast amounts of water from the upper lake began spilling into this basin,

44
00:03:34,681 --> 00:03:36,680
Niagara Falls was born.

45
00:03:36,681 --> 00:03:39,680
Thousands of years ago, I would think that when people saw the falls,

46
00:03:39,681 --> 00:03:44,680
they thought of it as being a source of power that was religious.

47
00:03:44,681 --> 00:03:49,680
Maybe they thought of it as being a manifestation of one of their spirits or gods.

48
00:03:49,681 --> 00:03:55,680
And the first missionaries who came to this area also thought that the Falls was one of the works of God.

49
00:03:55,681 --> 00:04:04,680
It showed his handiwork, his might, that he was able to conceive of something so awesome and created.

50
00:04:06,680 --> 00:04:13,680
The missionaries may have stood back, admired, and revered the Falls, but others wanted to utilize the power.

51
00:04:14,680 --> 00:04:27,540
In the mid-1600s, a Frenchman by the name of Jean Caire built a mill above the American Falls, near the American Rapids, and he used this mill to cut lumber.

52
00:04:27,541 --> 00:04:39,460
In fact, this mill was very useful at that time because it was the time of the French and Indian War, and they used the power from this mill to cut planks for warships.

53
00:04:39,461 --> 00:04:43,760
Even though Jean Caire's idea spurred interest in the area,

54
00:04:43,761 --> 00:04:47,500
little would be accomplished for 150 years.

55
00:04:47,501 --> 00:04:57,800
Niagara Falls was the most impressive, awesome, and sublime power source in the world.

56
00:04:58,800 --> 00:05:06,840
And in people's imagination, there was no other place that conveyed power to the extent of Niagara Falls.

57
00:05:06,841 --> 00:05:11,840
and everyone wanted to put this to use.

58
00:05:11,841 --> 00:05:15,840
By 1805, New York State officials were granting

59
00:05:15,841 --> 00:05:20,840
unlimited water diversion rights to various private power ventures.

60
00:05:20,841 --> 00:05:25,840
At a public auction, Judge Augustus Porter and his brother Peter purchased water rights

61
00:05:25,841 --> 00:05:30,840
to the American Eastern Rapids above and below the falls.

62
00:05:30,841 --> 00:05:34,840
It was basically the same problem they had hundreds of years earlier.

63
00:05:34,841 --> 00:05:40,440
There just wasn't sufficient technology to utilize all the power that could be generated by the falls.

64
00:05:41,880 --> 00:05:46,520
Smaller falls were more than sufficient and they were much easier to harness.

65
00:05:47,960 --> 00:05:53,880
Again, the Porter family offered free land to build a canal to anyone who would be interested,

66
00:05:54,520 --> 00:05:57,080
and they weren't really able to attract any investors.

67
00:05:58,520 --> 00:06:04,440
Various engineers and entrepreneurs tried to tame Niagara, none successfully.

68
00:06:04,840 --> 00:06:14,240
Until 1860, when the Niagara Falls Hydraulic Power and Manufacturing Company built a canal 35 feet wide and 8 feet deep.

69
00:06:15,080 --> 00:06:23,560
The canal transported water from the Niagara River above the falls to the mills below the falls, but failed to attract businesses.

70
00:06:24,960 --> 00:06:28,780
Soon after the canal was completed, the company went bankrupt.

71
00:06:28,781 --> 00:06:35,380
entrepreneur Horace Day took over and for the next 17 years worked sporadically on the canal,

72
00:06:35,381 --> 00:06:38,582
deepening it, widening it, and lengthening it.

73
00:06:38,583 --> 00:06:42,340
But he had only one customer and also went bankrupt in

74
00:06:42,341 --> 00:06:43,041
1877.

75
00:06:43,042 --> 00:06:50,540
Hydropower was first used to produce electricity at a plant on the Fox River near

76
00:06:50,541 --> 00:06:54,542
Appleton, Wisconsin on September 30th, 1882.

77
00:06:54,543 --> 00:06:58,180
But it wasn't until a visionary European

78
00:06:58,181 --> 00:07:05,180
manufacturer purchased Horace Day's canal that man would again attempt to utilize Niagara Falls' vast power.

79
00:07:05,181 --> 00:07:11,180
Jacob Scholkopf was a German immigrant who came to Buffalo in the mid-1850s.

80
00:07:11,181 --> 00:07:15,180
He was an affluent tanner who saw a good business opportunity.

81
00:07:15,181 --> 00:07:20,180
After he purchased the canal, he built a few mills there.

82
00:07:20,181 --> 00:07:24,180
He attracted the interest of other mill owners from Buffalo.

83
00:07:24,181 --> 00:07:30,540
Before long, there were grain mills there and pulp mills and other small manufacturing outfits,

84
00:07:30,980 --> 00:07:38,180
which was all well and fine, but even so, this still represented sort of an old way of harnessing power.

85
00:07:39,460 --> 00:07:46,500
Now, in the 1880s, an engineer, in fact, an engineer who worked for the Erie Canal, Thomas Evershed,

86
00:07:46,501 --> 00:07:54,820
put forth a proposal that kind of was a bridge between what Schalkopf did and what would be

87
00:07:54,821 --> 00:07:57,481
done at the Niagara Falls Power Company.

88
00:07:57,482 --> 00:08:00,740
He proposed building an inlet canal off the upper

89
00:08:00,741 --> 00:08:10,620
river which would in turn have individual mills located along it, but at a point above the falls,

90
00:08:10,621 --> 00:08:15,620
this canal would suddenly end and the water would be emptied underneath the city.

91
00:08:15,621 --> 00:08:19,620
That's the part that was kind of a unique take on it,

92
00:08:19,621 --> 00:08:23,620
the idea of having a tail-race tunnel built under the city.

93
00:08:23,621 --> 00:08:27,620
This concept drew a lot of interest.

94
00:08:27,621 --> 00:08:32,620
Some of the nation's leading financiers lined up behind Eversid's plan

95
00:08:32,621 --> 00:08:35,620
and were willing to invest their capital in it.

96
00:08:35,621 --> 00:08:49,100
These included Vanderbilts, Astors, Morgans, Edward Dean Adams, who later became the president of the Niagara Falls Power Company and its first historian.

97
00:08:50,240 --> 00:08:53,440
Big money behind it.

98
00:08:55,460 --> 00:09:02,800
Evershed's tunnel would generate 200,000 horsepower at the falls by turning gigantic water wheels.

99
00:09:02,801 --> 00:09:09,800
The tunnel would be two miles long and at least 160 feet deep.

100
00:09:09,801 --> 00:09:13,800
The problem with the Evershed plan was that it was rooted in the past.

101
00:09:13,801 --> 00:09:27,801
Evershed's plan wound up being transformed from a mill over wheel blueprint to a plan where power was distributed over long distances.

102
00:09:29,800 --> 00:09:41,760
Only when power could be efficiently distributed over long distances would Niagara Falls Power Project be a legitimate and viable venture.

103
00:09:42,840 --> 00:09:49,740
And this process involved the entire international engineering community.

104
00:09:50,220 --> 00:09:52,420
And it would involve electricity.

105
00:09:52,421 --> 00:09:55,020
The International Niagara Commission,

106
00:09:55,120 --> 00:09:58,460
made up of Niagara's most influential citizens,

107
00:09:58,560 --> 00:10:00,900
set up a contest that attracted entries

108
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:03,460
from the world's leading engineers,

109
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:06,540
all trying to harness the power of the falls

110
00:10:06,640 --> 00:10:10,080
and then send that power over long distances.

111
00:10:10,180 --> 00:10:13,500
Thomas Edison's direct current method of transmitting

112
00:10:13,600 --> 00:10:15,780
electricity was looked upon as a godsend

113
00:10:15,880 --> 00:10:18,380
for the power-hungry populace.

114
00:10:18,480 --> 00:10:21,680
But a mad genius from Croatia was about to threaten

115
00:10:21,681 --> 00:10:24,980
to threaten Edison's title as the world's premier

116
00:10:25,080 --> 00:10:28,380
electrical scientist.

117
00:10:28,480 --> 00:10:32,800
The year was 1884 when Croatian-born Nikola Tesla

118
00:10:32,900 --> 00:10:36,160
arrived in the United States with only four cents,

119
00:10:36,260 --> 00:10:38,660
a book of poetry, and a reference letter

120
00:10:38,760 --> 00:10:41,200
from a friend of Thomas Edison's.

121
00:10:41,300 --> 00:10:44,600
Still, Tesla was an up-and-coming scientist,

122
00:10:44,700 --> 00:10:49,000
known for his ability to concoct ideas solely in his mind

123
00:10:49,001 --> 00:10:51,260
and turn them from concepts to reality

124
00:10:51,340 --> 00:10:53,900
without even putting them on paper.

125
00:10:53,980 --> 00:10:57,440
He had worked for a European company headed by Edison

126
00:10:57,500 --> 00:11:00,420
and came to America to seek fame and fortune

127
00:11:00,480 --> 00:11:05,180
by taming the Great Falls of Niagara.

128
00:11:05,240 --> 00:11:07,880
The greatest names in engineering,

129
00:11:07,960 --> 00:11:11,860
Thomas Edison, Nikola Tesla,

130
00:11:11,920 --> 00:11:16,060
they all envisioned being the ones to harness

131
00:11:16,061 --> 00:11:18,860
the tremendous power potential of Niagara Falls.

132
00:11:18,861 --> 00:11:20,200
And this became a dream.

133
00:11:20,201 --> 00:11:24,760
Whoever could harness Niagara was going to make their fortune

134
00:11:24,761 --> 00:11:27,680
as well as make their legend.

135
00:11:28,500 --> 00:11:31,780
At this point, only Edison's direct current method

136
00:11:31,781 --> 00:11:34,200
had successfully transmitted electricity.

137
00:11:34,860 --> 00:11:38,340
But even the great inventor's plan had its shortcomings.

138
00:11:39,860 --> 00:11:43,200
Edison's direct current only sent power

139
00:11:43,201 --> 00:11:45,520
within a very limited range.

140
00:11:46,060 --> 00:11:51,060
Tesla, who developed alternating current,

141
00:11:51,061 --> 00:11:56,060
came up with a system that could send power over vast distances.

142
00:11:56,061 --> 00:12:02,060
And each believed that they had the correct system for sending power

143
00:12:02,061 --> 00:12:05,060
from Niagara Falls and harnessing it,

144
00:12:05,061 --> 00:12:08,060
and each wanted to be the first to do it, of course.

145
00:12:08,061 --> 00:12:11,060
Besides only transmitting very short distances,

146
00:12:11,061 --> 00:12:15,060
direct current flows through a conductor in one direction,

147
00:12:15,061 --> 00:12:17,100
with a steady flow of charges,

148
00:12:17,101 --> 00:12:21,240
while alternating current constantly reverses itself.

149
00:12:21,241 --> 00:12:24,340
This reversal pushes the current backward and forward

150
00:12:24,341 --> 00:12:26,980
through the wires connecting to the power source.

151
00:12:28,140 --> 00:12:31,640
The conflict was so intense that Edison and his supporters

152
00:12:31,641 --> 00:12:34,240
went around the country conducting a smear campaign

153
00:12:34,241 --> 00:12:37,220
against Tesla and his alternating current method

154
00:12:37,221 --> 00:12:38,920
of transmitting electricity.

155
00:12:40,220 --> 00:12:42,760
Thomas Edison initiated what became known

156
00:12:42,761 --> 00:12:45,000
the war of the currents.

157
00:12:45,001 --> 00:12:52,000
Edison was so determined to stave off the onslaught of AC

158
00:12:52,001 --> 00:12:59,820
that he began to begin a PR campaign to basically scare people.

159
00:12:59,821 --> 00:13:03,320
What he did is he began to call alternating current

160
00:13:03,321 --> 00:13:07,120
the executioner's current, the killing current.

161
00:13:07,121 --> 00:13:12,000
Edison himself, too, staged experiments

162
00:13:12,100 --> 00:13:14,060
with alternating current.

163
00:13:14,160 --> 00:13:17,600
In these experiments, he electrocuted dogs, cats,

164
00:13:17,700 --> 00:13:18,760
even an elephant.

165
00:13:18,860 --> 00:13:23,100
This PR campaign didn't succeed.

166
00:13:23,200 --> 00:13:25,840
The ability of Tesla's alternating current

167
00:13:25,940 --> 00:13:28,800
to transmit electricity great distances

168
00:13:28,900 --> 00:13:31,440
would allow the power companies to distribute more

169
00:13:31,540 --> 00:13:34,080
of the energy produced by the falls.

170
00:13:34,180 --> 00:13:36,720
It was this trade that convinced the Niagara Falls

171
00:13:36,721 --> 00:13:42,280
Power Company Board of Directors to award Tesla and George Westinghouse, Tesla's

172
00:13:42,281 --> 00:13:49,020
financier, the contract to build the first two generators at Niagara. The

173
00:13:49,021 --> 00:13:52,980
Niagara Falls Power Company moved ahead with construction of its mammoth

174
00:13:52,981 --> 00:14:00,960
underground tunnel. That task would take from 1892 to 1895. Using dynamite,

175
00:14:00,961 --> 00:14:06,600
pneumatically powered drills, steam shovels, and pickaxes, more than one

176
00:14:06,601 --> 00:14:13,600
1,000 workers labored around the clock during the three-year project to build a 6,700-foot-long,

177
00:14:13,740 --> 00:14:19,980
21-foot-high, and 18-foot-wide tunnel dubbed the greatest hydraulic tunnel in the world.

178
00:14:21,540 --> 00:14:24,980
Workers displaced 300,000 tons of rocks.

179
00:14:25,540 --> 00:14:32,440
20 million bricks lined the tunnel, and 2.5 million feet of oak and yellow pine were used to shore it up.

180
00:14:32,441 --> 00:14:39,440
Water drawn from the canal would move through the forebays, or intake reservoirs, and into the penstocks,

181
00:14:39,441 --> 00:14:45,440
large vertical pipes about eight feet in diameter, before entering the turbines.

182
00:14:45,441 --> 00:14:55,440
Once there, the water would spin a gigantic metal wheel, and then move up a 160-foot-long shaft that went up to the generator,

183
00:14:55,441 --> 00:14:59,440
which was located on the main floor of the powerhouse.

184
00:14:59,441 --> 00:15:04,440
The canal would produce 100,000 horsepower of electricity.

185
00:15:04,441 --> 00:15:11,440
Company executives were relieved that only 28 workers died during the course of the construction.

186
00:15:11,441 --> 00:15:15,440
The hydraulic canal was completed in 1893.

187
00:15:15,441 --> 00:15:22,440
Workers cut out a gigantic groove in the riverbank about 180 feet deep where the wheel pits were located.

188
00:15:22,441 --> 00:15:26,440
The wheel pits were, in essence, the basement of the powerhouse.

189
00:15:26,441 --> 00:15:34,440
During the summer of 1894, huge electrically powered cranes lowered the massive 29-ton double turbines,

190
00:15:34,441 --> 00:15:37,440
the largest in the world, into the wheel pits.

191
00:15:37,441 --> 00:15:43,440
The wheel pits were built into the ground, then flooring was filled in over the turbines and wheel pits.

192
00:15:43,441 --> 00:15:46,440
The powerhouse was built over that flooring.

193
00:15:46,441 --> 00:15:50,440
That was the challenge for architect Stanford White,

194
00:15:50,441 --> 00:15:57,340
Stanford White, who was called in to build a powerhouse capable of delivering a much more modest 15,000 horsepower.

195
00:15:58,180 --> 00:16:05,320
The Niagara Falls Power Company powerhouse was far different than any other power plant in the country.

196
00:16:06,580 --> 00:16:10,580
Most power plants were makeshift structures, very unsightly.

197
00:16:11,340 --> 00:16:16,680
There were coal piles and ash heaps to the sides of them.

198
00:16:16,681 --> 00:16:19,580
They were grimy places.

199
00:16:19,960 --> 00:16:21,340
They were dangerous places.

200
00:16:22,100 --> 00:16:27,649
Edward Dean Adams was handpicked by Niagara Falls Power Company board member J.P.

201
00:16:27,650 --> 00:16:28,350
Morgan

202
00:16:28,351 --> 00:16:32,500
to run the company and oversee the construction of this grand powerhouse.

203
00:16:33,480 --> 00:16:38,540
Edward Dean Adams said he wanted this place, as he put it, to inspire awe.

204
00:16:39,200 --> 00:16:44,380
He wanted people to look at it and to think, wow, something really important is happening there.

205
00:16:44,381 --> 00:16:49,820
And I like to compare it to being almost like a technological cathedral.

206
00:16:50,820 --> 00:16:55,560
It's a place that's built in a very grand style

207
00:16:55,561 --> 00:17:00,620
because something very important, something very unique was taking place there.

208
00:17:01,100 --> 00:17:03,100
Now it's become commonplace to us,

209
00:17:03,260 --> 00:17:08,500
but back then it was something very, very impressive.

210
00:17:09,040 --> 00:17:11,620
The powerhouse was a work in progress.

211
00:17:11,621 --> 00:17:14,560
originally 140 feet long.

212
00:17:14,561 --> 00:17:18,520
By 1903, it eventually grew to 450 feet long

213
00:17:18,521 --> 00:17:21,040
and consisted of 11 generators.

214
00:17:22,100 --> 00:17:25,140
The renowned architecture firm of McKim, Mead & White

215
00:17:25,141 --> 00:17:27,400
designed the switchboard control panel

216
00:17:27,401 --> 00:17:29,560
with Italian marble around the steps.

217
00:17:30,720 --> 00:17:33,120
Other interior components of the powerhouse

218
00:17:33,121 --> 00:17:36,900
were impressive enough to move great writers of the time.

219
00:17:36,901 --> 00:17:41,900
The powerhouse, of course, housed the Niagara dynamos.

220
00:17:42,900 --> 00:17:47,900
And really what the steam locomotive was to the steam era,

221
00:17:48,900 --> 00:17:51,900
the dynamo was to the electric age.

222
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:56,900
This was the mystical power source.

223
00:17:57,900 --> 00:18:04,900
And these whirling structures inside the powerhouse

224
00:18:04,901 --> 00:18:10,900
the powerhouse had a capability of 5,000 horsepower, which today wouldn't be very much,

225
00:18:10,901 --> 00:18:17,900
but back in this era, a machine of 100 horsepower was considered quite large.

226
00:18:17,901 --> 00:18:24,900
These mystical dynamos were remarkable really for the fact that they were so unremarkable.

227
00:18:24,901 --> 00:18:32,900
The writer H.G. Wells talked about them as slumbering black monsters

228
00:18:32,901 --> 00:18:36,900
monsters and big whirling tops.

229
00:18:36,901 --> 00:18:39,100
Water enters the turbines.

230
00:18:39,101 --> 00:18:41,580
A shaft connects the turbine to a rotor,

231
00:18:41,581 --> 00:18:43,460
which is part of the generator.

232
00:18:43,461 --> 00:18:45,780
As the turbine spins below the generator,

233
00:18:45,781 --> 00:18:47,600
it turns the shaft and rotor.

234
00:18:47,601 --> 00:18:49,640
To produce electricity, the generator

235
00:18:49,641 --> 00:18:52,020
needs magnets and copper wiring.

236
00:18:52,021 --> 00:18:54,460
The magnets are affixed to the rotor's rim.

237
00:18:54,461 --> 00:18:57,800
The rim sits inside the stator, which surrounds the rotor

238
00:18:57,801 --> 00:19:00,520
with almost 400 copper coils.

239
00:19:00,521 --> 00:19:02,760
The rotor spins inside the stator,

240
00:19:02,761 --> 00:19:05,300
producing electricity in the copper coils.

241
00:19:07,400 --> 00:19:09,280
Even with stunning architecture

242
00:19:09,281 --> 00:19:10,900
and a nod to Niagara's past,

243
00:19:10,901 --> 00:19:12,940
as well as a glimpse into the future,

244
00:19:12,941 --> 00:19:15,040
Niagara's hydroelectric power plants

245
00:19:15,041 --> 00:19:17,740
didn't fully capture the imagination of the public

246
00:19:17,741 --> 00:19:19,040
until 1901.

247
00:19:20,320 --> 00:19:23,020
The Pan American Exposition ran for six months

248
00:19:23,021 --> 00:19:24,480
at the Buffalo Fairgrounds,

249
00:19:24,481 --> 00:19:27,400
only 20 miles from Niagara Falls.

250
00:19:27,401 --> 00:19:28,600
The highlight of the expo

251
00:19:28,601 --> 00:19:32,140
was the first long-distance transmission of electricity,

252
00:19:32,141 --> 00:19:35,840
thanks entirely to power produced from the falls.

253
00:19:35,841 --> 00:19:37,900
This display of power would forever

254
00:19:37,901 --> 00:19:40,180
alter the future of Niagara Falls.

255
00:19:45,160 --> 00:19:47,640
Niagara power companies were able to attract

256
00:19:47,641 --> 00:19:51,520
all kinds of industries to Niagara with cheap electricity

257
00:19:51,521 --> 00:19:56,300
and the promise of free land and housing for their employees.

258
00:19:56,301 --> 00:20:01,300
In the 1890s, once the Niagara Falls Power Company's power project was underway,

259
00:20:01,301 --> 00:20:06,300
companies in different parts of the nation heard about this and they thought,

260
00:20:06,301 --> 00:20:09,300
well, maybe we should look into Niagara Falls.

261
00:20:09,301 --> 00:20:15,300
There were, of course, some small factories in Niagara Falls, but nothing very large scale,

262
00:20:15,301 --> 00:20:20,300
nothing really revolutionary as far as its industrial process.

263
00:20:20,301 --> 00:20:27,300
One of the first ones to come to Niagara Falls was the Pittsburgh Reduction Company.

264
00:20:27,301 --> 00:20:32,300
They needed lots of power and cheap power to create a product.

265
00:20:32,301 --> 00:20:41,300
The Pittsburgh Reduction Company, later renamed Alcoa, was just one of many companies that welcomed the move to Niagara Falls.

266
00:20:41,301 --> 00:20:48,300
Edward Acheson's Graphite Company, which produced Carborundum, also took advantage of Niagara's cheap power.

267
00:20:48,301 --> 00:20:55,300
But in Acheson's case, the move was made over the objections of his investors.

268
00:20:55,301 --> 00:21:01,300
When he announced to his investors that he was going to move his plant to Niagara Falls,

269
00:21:01,301 --> 00:21:05,300
some were so outraged and thought this was such a wild idea that they left the company

270
00:21:05,301 --> 00:21:08,300
and basically took their toys home with them.

271
00:21:08,301 --> 00:21:11,300
They just pulled out of the company.

272
00:21:11,301 --> 00:21:15,300
But within the next few years, Acheson showed what a wise choice it was.

273
00:21:15,301 --> 00:21:17,540
the Carborundum Company mushroomed.

274
00:21:17,541 --> 00:21:20,600
It became a gigantic industry.

275
00:21:20,601 --> 00:21:24,340
The turn of the century saw Niagara Falls become a mecca

276
00:21:24,341 --> 00:21:27,740
as paper mills, cereal companies like Henry Perky's

277
00:21:27,741 --> 00:21:30,740
Shredded Wheat Biscuit Company and Camp Gillette's

278
00:21:30,741 --> 00:21:34,760
Safety Razor Company appeared on both sides of the border.

279
00:21:34,761 --> 00:21:37,760
Interest in utilizing the power of the falls had always

280
00:21:37,761 --> 00:21:41,100
existed on the Canadian side, but until the success of the

281
00:21:41,101 --> 00:21:44,600
American efforts was evident, the Canadians were content to

282
00:21:44,601 --> 00:21:47,100
intend to take a wait-and-see attitude.

283
00:21:47,101 --> 00:21:49,760
Once they were convinced of Niagara's promise,

284
00:21:49,761 --> 00:21:52,300
Canadian interests soared.

285
00:21:52,301 --> 00:21:55,080
There were three power companies developing

286
00:21:55,081 --> 00:21:58,120
the potential of Niagara Falls on the Canadian side.

287
00:21:58,121 --> 00:21:59,900
There was the Toronto Power Company,

288
00:21:59,901 --> 00:22:02,860
or otherwise known as the Electrical Development Company,

289
00:22:02,861 --> 00:22:06,040
which was a private Canadian company.

290
00:22:06,041 --> 00:22:08,720
They were established to develop power in Niagara Falls

291
00:22:08,721 --> 00:22:10,720
and transmit it to Toronto.

292
00:22:10,721 --> 00:22:14,841
The other company, Canadian Niagara, was a U.S.

293
00:22:14,842 --> 00:22:18,260
company, and they are still here today.

294
00:22:18,900 --> 00:22:25,480
And the third company was Ontario Power, which was, again, a private American company

295
00:22:25,481 --> 00:22:29,480
that was developing their plant at the base of the falls.

296
00:22:29,481 --> 00:22:33,460
All three of these plants were under construction at the same time, and all produced power,

297
00:22:33,600 --> 00:22:35,860
their first power around 1905, 1906.

298
00:22:35,861 --> 00:22:43,100
The Canadian power plants were located inside a state park alongside the natural wonder of Niagara Falls,

299
00:22:43,300 --> 00:22:45,600
which infuriated naturalists.

300
00:22:46,320 --> 00:22:51,140
There was certainly a desire in the part of the government to preserve the beauty of the falls

301
00:22:51,141 --> 00:22:54,380
and preserve it as a free tourist attraction.

302
00:22:54,600 --> 00:22:58,540
Certainly one of the fears of the Parks Commission was having industrial plants in the park.

303
00:22:58,541 --> 00:23:06,160
So when you see these older plants, their architecture is really quite attractive.

304
00:23:07,060 --> 00:23:13,440
The developers of the company's power plants wanted to make sure that they could demonstrate

305
00:23:13,441 --> 00:23:18,660
that, yes, you could build an attractive power plant and have it at the falls without detracting from the natural beauty.

306
00:23:19,360 --> 00:23:26,280
Since 1892, the Edward Dean Adams Station, previously known as Powerhouse No. 1,

307
00:23:26,281 --> 00:23:29,560
has operated on the American side of the falls.

308
00:23:30,120 --> 00:23:34,520
Then, in 1900, Canadian Niagara started construction on this plant,

309
00:23:34,900 --> 00:23:38,380
the William Rankin Generating Station, which is still in use today.

310
00:23:39,180 --> 00:23:42,640
It was the first power plant built on the Canadian side of the falls,

311
00:23:43,060 --> 00:23:46,780
and in grandeur followed the lead of the American power plants.

312
00:23:48,140 --> 00:23:55,460
Architecturally, the building itself stands out as a marvel of its time.

313
00:23:55,461 --> 00:23:57,260
The marble was imported from Italy.

314
00:23:57,800 --> 00:24:02,620
The clay tiles that were used from the roof were built to rigid structure.

315
00:24:03,320 --> 00:24:07,060
Everything was overbuilt with the aesthetics in mind also.

316
00:24:07,180 --> 00:24:11,220
So there were certain criterias that were required to build this plant.

317
00:24:11,360 --> 00:24:14,220
You would really never know that it is a hydroelectric plant

318
00:24:14,221 --> 00:24:19,300
because you do see a nice calming effect of what looks like a pond in front of a building,

319
00:24:19,440 --> 00:24:20,660
but really that is the forebay.

320
00:24:20,661 --> 00:24:24,260
By 1905, the Niagara Falls Power Company

321
00:24:24,360 --> 00:24:27,240
had two power stations in operation,

322
00:24:27,340 --> 00:24:29,700
while three existed on the Canadian side.

323
00:24:29,800 --> 00:24:32,180
There was so much power development going on

324
00:24:32,280 --> 00:24:36,180
at Niagara Falls, so many competing interests,

325
00:24:36,280 --> 00:24:39,780
and also apparently such a demand for power

326
00:24:39,880 --> 00:24:42,380
that a lot of people in the US and Canada

327
00:24:42,480 --> 00:24:46,120
became concerned that perhaps too much water

328
00:24:46,220 --> 00:24:49,280
would be diverted from the river and the falls would practically

329
00:24:49,281 --> 00:24:51,480
practically fade into oblivion.

330
00:24:51,481 --> 00:24:53,560
The United States and Canada signed

331
00:24:53,561 --> 00:24:56,880
an international treaty in 1907

332
00:24:56,881 --> 00:24:59,520
that strictly regulated the amount of water

333
00:24:59,521 --> 00:25:02,000
that could be diverted by the two countries.

334
00:25:04,100 --> 00:25:06,460
Power companies that were already established

335
00:25:06,461 --> 00:25:08,760
divided up each nation's portion.

336
00:25:08,761 --> 00:25:11,760
I myself think if that treaty had not been signed,

337
00:25:11,761 --> 00:25:14,700
most of the water would indeed be going through penstocks

338
00:25:14,701 --> 00:25:16,480
instead of over the falls,

339
00:25:16,481 --> 00:25:21,920
because each of the power companies had plans for further expansion, every single one.

340
00:25:23,060 --> 00:25:28,060
And as time progressed, the demand for power did grow,

341
00:25:28,440 --> 00:25:30,640
and I'm sure they would have found a market for it.

342
00:25:32,760 --> 00:25:36,360
By 1918, the demand for more power did grow,

343
00:25:36,560 --> 00:25:39,500
but not due to more industry coming to Niagara.

344
00:25:40,120 --> 00:25:44,840
With the advent of World War I, international treaty regulations were eased,

345
00:25:44,841 --> 00:25:48,840
and the power companies were able to increase their energy output.

346
00:25:48,940 --> 00:25:51,940
At the newly constructed American-based Shulkoff plant,

347
00:25:52,040 --> 00:25:54,580
the war effort brought about significant achievements

348
00:25:54,680 --> 00:25:57,780
in power plant production, including moving the power plant

349
00:25:57,880 --> 00:26:01,320
about eight miles farther away from the falls.

350
00:26:01,420 --> 00:26:03,660
It used a head of water.

351
00:26:03,760 --> 00:26:07,360
In other words, the water dropped from a greater height

352
00:26:07,460 --> 00:26:11,300
than it did at the Niagara Falls Power Company's power station.

353
00:26:11,400 --> 00:26:13,360
That in itself enabled the power station

354
00:26:13,361 --> 00:26:19,360
to generate more power, but also larger turbines had been designed by then,

355
00:26:19,361 --> 00:26:26,360
turbines that could withstand the force of that type of drop, that velocity of water fall.

356
00:26:26,361 --> 00:26:30,360
The generators were built directly on top of the turbines.

357
00:26:30,361 --> 00:26:33,360
They weren't connected by a long vertical shaft.

358
00:26:33,361 --> 00:26:38,360
They were very close in placement.

359
00:26:38,361 --> 00:26:41,600
That, along with a lot of other engineering innovations,

360
00:26:41,601 --> 00:26:46,800
made it possible for that power station to generate huge amounts of power,

361
00:26:47,020 --> 00:26:49,780
again making it the world's leading power generator.

362
00:26:50,400 --> 00:26:56,000
On the Canadian side, creative problem-solving and improving technology were taking place.

363
00:26:56,580 --> 00:27:00,560
The Queenston, Ontario power plant, owned by Ontario Hydro,

364
00:27:00,680 --> 00:27:04,660
actually called for building a canal that would reverse the flow of the Welland River,

365
00:27:04,661 --> 00:27:08,660
about one mile above the Niagara River and Niagara Falls.

366
00:27:10,260 --> 00:27:13,780
The canal certainly was an engineering feat.

367
00:27:14,480 --> 00:27:16,620
The construction started in 1917,

368
00:27:17,040 --> 00:27:20,540
and it wasn't completed until 1921.

369
00:27:21,000 --> 00:27:22,320
It's really a period of four years,

370
00:27:22,520 --> 00:27:27,760
intensive effort, horsepower, shovel power, human power,

371
00:27:28,240 --> 00:27:30,440
and you see some of the photographs,

372
00:27:30,441 --> 00:27:36,980
you know, blasting and very real, large, old steam shovels powered by steam.

373
00:27:37,440 --> 00:27:44,040
So the labor was intensive, but again, it was the state-of-the-art construction techniques of the day.

374
00:27:44,540 --> 00:27:48,760
When completed, the Queenston Canal was nearly 13 miles long

375
00:27:48,761 --> 00:27:52,900
and could carry up to 600 cubic meters of water per second.

376
00:27:53,600 --> 00:27:59,240
By 1921, a massive power plant was needed to house and harness the river's energy.

377
00:27:59,241 --> 00:28:06,580
It was a major development, seen as something that was so big that construction was actually put to a public referendum.

378
00:28:07,660 --> 00:28:11,280
And that referendum was approved in New Year's Day 1917.

379
00:28:12,440 --> 00:28:15,480
The plant would have 10 generators.

380
00:28:16,180 --> 00:28:20,360
The total capacity of the plant would be 450,000 kilowatts.

381
00:28:20,880 --> 00:28:26,380
It was seen as supplying the power, electric power, to the province of Ontario to the turn of the century.

382
00:28:26,381 --> 00:28:31,380
When it was completed in 1925, it was indeed the largest power plant in the world.

383
00:28:32,320 --> 00:28:35,760
While the Niagara power plants continued to grow in size,

384
00:28:35,880 --> 00:28:39,920
the technologies developed during World War I would change Niagara's role

385
00:28:39,921 --> 00:28:42,560
as the world's leading hydroelectricity producer.

386
00:28:43,560 --> 00:28:48,120
In fact, mammoth hydroelectric power plants, larger than anything seen at Niagara,

387
00:28:48,500 --> 00:28:51,740
started cropping up in New York City, Chicago, and Arizona.

388
00:28:51,741 --> 00:28:55,740
These new power plants combined large building sites,

389
00:28:55,741 --> 00:28:58,740
upgraded technology, and government involvement,

390
00:28:58,741 --> 00:29:03,740
practically making Niagara's turn-of-the-century powerhouses obsolete.

391
00:29:03,741 --> 00:29:09,740
In early 1949, Alcoa, at one time the world's largest user of electricity,

392
00:29:09,741 --> 00:29:12,740
shut down its Niagara plant and moved south.

393
00:29:12,741 --> 00:29:16,740
It would take a tragedy for Niagara to regain its place

394
00:29:16,741 --> 00:29:20,740
as the leader of hydroelectric power.

395
00:29:20,741 --> 00:29:27,740
In the late 1940s, the United States and Canada anticipated

396
00:29:27,840 --> 00:29:32,680
the expiration of the treaty that regulated the amount of water

397
00:29:32,780 --> 00:29:36,520
each country could use for hydroelectric power.

398
00:29:36,620 --> 00:29:38,860
Both countries saw this as an opportunity

399
00:29:38,960 --> 00:29:41,660
to expand their take of the Niagara River,

400
00:29:41,760 --> 00:29:44,600
thus increasing their energy output.

401
00:29:44,700 --> 00:29:48,200
The treaty in place since 1907 allowed Canada to be

402
00:29:48,201 --> 00:29:53,680
in place since 1907 allowed Canada to draw 56,500 cubic feet

403
00:29:53,780 --> 00:29:56,940
of water per second from the falls,

404
00:29:57,040 --> 00:30:01,520
while the United States could draw 32,500 cubic feet of water

405
00:30:01,620 --> 00:30:03,080
a second.

406
00:30:03,180 --> 00:30:05,580
The new treaty signed in September 1950

407
00:30:05,680 --> 00:30:08,560
declared that once the scenic protection of the falls

408
00:30:08,660 --> 00:30:11,920
was guaranteed, all the water in the Niagara River

409
00:30:12,020 --> 00:30:14,020
could be used to develop power.

410
00:30:14,120 --> 00:30:17,760
This meant that not less than 100,000 cubic feet of water

411
00:30:17,761 --> 00:30:22,360
must be allowed to flow over the American and Canadian falls

412
00:30:22,361 --> 00:30:25,560
during the summer months, while the flow would be reduced

413
00:30:25,561 --> 00:30:29,300
to 50,000 cubic feet between November and April.

414
00:30:29,301 --> 00:30:31,800
Only four days after the treaty was signed,

415
00:30:31,801 --> 00:30:36,320
Ontario Hydro had plans underway for Sir Adam Beck Station

416
00:30:36,321 --> 00:30:37,320
Number Two.

417
00:30:41,260 --> 00:30:43,420
Construction started in 1950.

418
00:30:43,421 --> 00:30:47,060
The diversion of water from above the falls to the power plants

419
00:30:47,061 --> 00:30:52,940
power plants downstream involved the construction of two tunnels approximately 40 feet in diameter

420
00:30:52,941 --> 00:30:53,641
each.

421
00:30:53,642 --> 00:30:59,660
The construction of the tunnels was essentially a mining technique, drill and blast,

422
00:30:59,661 --> 00:31:01,753
and pour concrete on the spot.

423
00:31:01,754 --> 00:31:05,660
There was considerable disruption in throughout the city

424
00:31:05,661 --> 00:31:10,560
in Niagara Falls over a period of four years where material would be brought to the surface

425
00:31:10,561 --> 00:31:16,740
through various service shafts throughout the city. The Beck Station No. 2 was built

426
00:31:16,741 --> 00:31:21,280
only a few yards upstream from Beck Station Number One.

427
00:31:21,281 --> 00:31:24,980
When completed, it produced 700,000 horsepower,

428
00:31:24,981 --> 00:31:28,920
and it cost $157 million.

429
00:31:28,921 --> 00:31:31,860
On the American side, the New York State Power Authority

430
00:31:31,861 --> 00:31:36,160
wanted to follow Canada's lead and build a state-of-the-art plant.

431
00:31:36,161 --> 00:31:38,660
But after six years of political wrangling,

432
00:31:38,661 --> 00:31:42,900
the Power Authority's plans were still on the drawing board.

433
00:31:42,901 --> 00:31:45,500
That is, until a power plant collapsed

434
00:31:45,501 --> 00:31:51,800
on the American side of the Niagara Gorge on June 7, 1956.

435
00:31:55,740 --> 00:32:00,540
The 1924 Sholkoff plant was the largest electricity provider

436
00:32:00,541 --> 00:32:02,380
for the eastern seaboard.

437
00:32:02,381 --> 00:32:04,460
Forty years after the accident,

438
00:32:04,461 --> 00:32:08,860
there is still no single explanation for the Sholkoff disaster,

439
00:32:08,861 --> 00:32:13,500
but a major factor appears to be connected to an earthquake ten years earlier.

440
00:32:13,501 --> 00:32:18,300
Back in the early 40s, I remember being in the lower part

441
00:32:18,301 --> 00:32:22,540
of the last unit they built, making measurements.

442
00:32:22,541 --> 00:32:26,840
And there was a big indication that there was a lot

443
00:32:26,841 --> 00:32:29,120
of deformation down in there.

444
00:32:29,121 --> 00:32:31,980
There were also several leaks along the back wall

445
00:32:31,981 --> 00:32:33,520
of the powerhouse.

446
00:32:33,521 --> 00:32:36,860
But it was several years before these problems grew larger

447
00:32:36,861 --> 00:32:38,920
and needed to be addressed.

448
00:32:38,921 --> 00:32:42,620
So that sometime in early 1956,

449
00:32:42,621 --> 00:32:47,220
It was decided that we were going to try and stop those leaks.

450
00:32:48,820 --> 00:32:55,360
And we were going to drill a number of holes in a high bank

451
00:32:55,361 --> 00:33:04,540
and pump grout down these holes to try and seal up this leakage.

452
00:33:05,240 --> 00:33:08,800
A team of engineers drilled holes 300 feet deep

453
00:33:08,801 --> 00:33:11,500
in order to reach the forebay's concrete walls,

454
00:33:11,501 --> 00:33:13,700
which had developed fissures.

455
00:33:13,800 --> 00:33:15,880
There were three people at the power plant

456
00:33:15,980 --> 00:33:18,080
when the accident occurred.

457
00:33:18,180 --> 00:33:21,080
Machinist Norman Draper was the only fatality.

458
00:33:25,180 --> 00:33:28,720
Since the early 1950s, a consortium of five power

459
00:33:28,820 --> 00:33:31,880
companies had been negotiating to build a new power station

460
00:33:31,980 --> 00:33:34,360
near Niagara University to replace

461
00:33:34,460 --> 00:33:37,160
the outdated Sholkoff plant.

462
00:33:37,260 --> 00:33:40,260
The Sholkoff disaster was the impetus for the US government

463
00:33:40,261 --> 00:33:42,060
government to get involved.

464
00:33:42,061 --> 00:33:48,440
On August 21, 1957, President Dwight Eisenhower signed the Niagara Redevelopment Act, which

465
00:33:48,441 --> 00:33:53,480
allowed the New York Power Authority to fully exploit the United States' share of the Niagara

466
00:33:53,481 --> 00:33:56,820
River's hydroelectric power potential.

467
00:33:56,821 --> 00:34:01,620
It would become the largest engineering project in the United States at that time, and one

468
00:34:01,621 --> 00:34:03,820
of the largest in the world.

469
00:34:03,821 --> 00:34:09,260
The New York Power Authority purchased 200 acres of land from Niagara University to build

470
00:34:09,261 --> 00:34:10,920
the new power plant.

471
00:34:11,020 --> 00:34:12,660
Initially, there was a lot of opposition

472
00:34:12,760 --> 00:34:16,400
to the idea of the government operating a power station.

473
00:34:16,500 --> 00:34:21,640
But nonetheless, the fact that there was an emergency

474
00:34:21,740 --> 00:34:26,340
made people feel that things had to happen quickly.

475
00:34:26,440 --> 00:34:29,180
The power authority started construction of its tunnel,

476
00:34:29,280 --> 00:34:31,380
even though the state agency was embroiled

477
00:34:31,480 --> 00:34:34,620
in a lengthy battle with the Tuscarora Indians,

478
00:34:34,720 --> 00:34:37,160
who inhabited the 6,200 acres of land

479
00:34:37,161 --> 00:34:40,840
next to the land purchased by the Power Authority.

480
00:34:40,841 --> 00:34:44,260
While negotiations between the Tuscarora and Power Authority

481
00:34:44,261 --> 00:34:47,980
dragged on, construction also started on the Robert Moses

482
00:34:47,981 --> 00:34:51,600
plant in the spring of 1958.

483
00:34:51,601 --> 00:34:53,980
I can remember the blasting.

484
00:34:53,981 --> 00:34:57,520
I mean, we have cracks in our walls in our home

485
00:34:57,521 --> 00:34:58,980
as a result of the blasting.

486
00:34:58,981 --> 00:35:02,680
More than 100,000 workers braved bitter winters

487
00:35:02,681 --> 00:35:05,760
and humid summers to meet the state's deadline.

488
00:35:05,761 --> 00:35:07,920
Eight temporary bridges were constructed,

489
00:35:07,921 --> 00:35:11,600
and five million yards of earth and rock had to be removed.

490
00:35:11,601 --> 00:35:15,100
Construction of the power plant forced detours of streets,

491
00:35:15,101 --> 00:35:16,880
roads, and highways.

492
00:35:16,881 --> 00:35:19,540
Even houses were placed on trailers

493
00:35:19,541 --> 00:35:23,580
and moved three miles away to a newly erected subdivision.

494
00:35:23,581 --> 00:35:26,940
While all this was going on, the battle between the Tuscarora

495
00:35:26,941 --> 00:35:29,920
and the state of New York, which oversaw the power authority,

496
00:35:29,921 --> 00:35:33,280
continued all the way to the United States Supreme Court.

497
00:35:33,281 --> 00:35:37,920
Finally, on March 7, 1960, the court handed down its decision.

498
00:35:38,620 --> 00:35:43,560
By a 6-3 vote, the court held that the tribe had never acquired their land by treaty,

499
00:35:44,060 --> 00:35:45,560
but by gift and purchase.

500
00:35:46,020 --> 00:35:50,500
This meant they had no legal right to receive any compensation for the land.

501
00:35:50,501 --> 00:35:52,880
With that decision, the power authority,

502
00:35:53,240 --> 00:35:55,760
under the leadership of its chairman, Robert Moses,

503
00:35:56,260 --> 00:35:59,340
moved swiftly to make their 1961 deadline.

504
00:35:59,341 --> 00:36:06,340
The construction of the Robert Moses power plant was a gigantic undertaking.

505
00:36:06,341 --> 00:36:12,340
There were massive dump trucks and massive cranes and bulldozers and explosives.

506
00:36:12,341 --> 00:36:17,340
Nonetheless, of course, it was a civil engineering feat.

507
00:36:17,341 --> 00:36:26,340
The construction of the power station called not only for a portion of the gorge wall to be removed,

508
00:36:26,341 --> 00:36:29,540
to enable the placement of a power station there,

509
00:36:29,840 --> 00:36:33,880
but it also required the excavation of a deep forebay,

510
00:36:34,520 --> 00:36:40,620
and also it called for two tunnels to be dug under the city of Niagara Falls

511
00:36:40,621 --> 00:36:42,040
to be blasted away.

512
00:36:42,560 --> 00:36:46,160
More than 3.5 million cubic yards of concrete was poured.

513
00:36:46,660 --> 00:36:49,420
Almost 10 million cubic yards of earth was excavated,

514
00:36:49,421 --> 00:36:53,500
and 24.5 million cubic yards of rock was displaced

515
00:36:53,501 --> 00:36:55,580
during the three-year construction period.

516
00:36:55,581 --> 00:36:59,380
Construction on that site went on night and day.

517
00:36:59,480 --> 00:37:01,420
It never really stopped.

518
00:37:01,520 --> 00:37:05,320
They used gigantic spotlights and just kept blasting rock

519
00:37:05,420 --> 00:37:07,920
and pouring concrete, placing steel,

520
00:37:08,020 --> 00:37:10,620
and it was a big venture.

521
00:37:10,720 --> 00:37:15,160
On January 28, 1961, the Robert Moses Generating Plant

522
00:37:15,260 --> 00:37:18,060
and the Lewiston Pump Generation Plant opened.

523
00:37:18,160 --> 00:37:23,240
The power project cost a staggering $757 million

524
00:37:23,241 --> 00:37:27,640
and was the largest hydropower facility in the Western world.

525
00:37:27,740 --> 00:37:32,440
The Moses plant is mammoth, almost 2,000 feet long,

526
00:37:32,540 --> 00:37:36,880
38 stories high, and 580 feet wide.

527
00:37:36,980 --> 00:37:40,120
Its 13 turbines are equally impressive.

528
00:37:40,220 --> 00:37:43,360
Each turbine is made up of stainless steel shafts,

529
00:37:43,460 --> 00:37:46,960
each 50 inches in diameter and 32 feet long,

530
00:37:47,060 --> 00:37:49,860
divided into two 16-foot sections.

531
00:37:49,861 --> 00:37:53,900
The sections are joined with 18 6 1⁄2-inch bolts

532
00:37:53,901 --> 00:37:57,040
that are stretched across the width of the shaft.

533
00:37:57,041 --> 00:38:00,000
With the new power plants came new technology.

534
00:38:00,001 --> 00:38:03,040
The Moses and Lewiston plants employed a new technique

535
00:38:03,041 --> 00:38:07,920
to transport water from the river to the 1,900-acre reservoir.

536
00:38:07,921 --> 00:38:09,340
The tunnel that delivers the water,

537
00:38:09,341 --> 00:38:11,720
or I should say the tunnels, we refer to them as the conduits

538
00:38:11,721 --> 00:38:14,880
that bring the water from the Upper Niagara River,

539
00:38:14,881 --> 00:38:18,660
they were completed in a method called cut and cover.

540
00:38:18,661 --> 00:38:22,160
two trenches were initially dug in the ground,

541
00:38:22,161 --> 00:38:25,160
and then they were taken and lined with concrete.

542
00:38:25,161 --> 00:38:29,360
And the thickness is, I mean, I know the walls are like two and a half feet thick,

543
00:38:29,361 --> 00:38:33,960
the floor is four foot thick, and the arch itself is about five foot thick.

544
00:38:33,961 --> 00:38:38,960
And this was all done in forty foot sections, and we just moved right down the entire length

545
00:38:38,961 --> 00:38:41,360
to complete the construction of these two conduits.

546
00:38:41,361 --> 00:38:46,960
And then it was backfilled right over with overburden, topsoil,

547
00:38:46,961 --> 00:38:49,800
and you wouldn't even know that they're there right now.

548
00:38:49,801 --> 00:38:52,200
Maintaining the conduits has also pushed

549
00:38:52,201 --> 00:38:54,100
the technological envelope.

550
00:38:54,101 --> 00:38:56,900
If you think about inspecting a four-mile tunnel

551
00:38:56,901 --> 00:38:58,740
underwater, how do you do it?

552
00:38:58,741 --> 00:39:00,980
It's more than you could do with a manned diver.

553
00:39:00,981 --> 00:39:03,840
So we've actually done it twice.

554
00:39:03,841 --> 00:39:07,880
We've gone in at each end, and we put a manned submersible in.

555
00:39:07,881 --> 00:39:11,780
And this is a tethered submarine that an individual will be in,

556
00:39:11,781 --> 00:39:15,180
and he goes in from each end about two miles,

557
00:39:15,181 --> 00:39:23,320
And we do a visual inspection plus a camera inspection of the walls, the ceiling, and the floor to look for any problems.

558
00:39:23,500 --> 00:39:26,000
We went out and had a specially designed cable built.

559
00:39:26,140 --> 00:39:27,120
It's quite interesting.

560
00:39:27,640 --> 00:39:31,800
It's a cable which has the mechanical strength necessary to pull the submarine back if it had to.

561
00:39:31,801 --> 00:39:36,500
It has electrical feed, obviously, for the electrical equipment on board.

562
00:39:36,620 --> 00:39:40,000
And it has fiber optics in the center to bring the video back.

563
00:39:40,001 --> 00:39:44,000
But that's only half the battle in turning water from the

564
00:39:44,001 --> 00:39:48,000
Niagara River into electricity for the eastern seaboard.

565
00:39:48,001 --> 00:39:52,000
If the canal walls are safe, water that's diverted from the

566
00:39:52,001 --> 00:39:55,000
Niagara River will flow into penstocks.

567
00:39:55,001 --> 00:39:58,000
We have 24-foot diameter penstocks, which brings the water

568
00:39:58,001 --> 00:40:02,000
down from the forebay down to the generator itself.

569
00:40:02,001 --> 00:40:06,000
The generator sits down here in the main powerhouse, where the

570
00:40:06,001 --> 00:40:10,500
where the turbine will spin inside a scroll case.

571
00:40:10,501 --> 00:40:14,100
The water that's been delivered from the 24-foot diameter pen stack

572
00:40:14,101 --> 00:40:19,100
passes through the scroll case, forcing the water into a turbine.

573
00:40:19,101 --> 00:40:25,540
The turbine acts as a water wheel does, turning the shaft of the generator.

574
00:40:25,541 --> 00:40:30,000
From there the power flows up through underground conduits up into the switch yard.

575
00:40:30,001 --> 00:40:34,400
And from the switch yard it flows out on 21 different transmission lines

576
00:40:34,401 --> 00:40:36,100
to various parts of the state.

577
00:40:36,101 --> 00:40:38,340
The advent of the state-of-the-art Niagara Power

578
00:40:38,341 --> 00:40:42,340
Project was the death blow for the turn of the century plants.

579
00:40:42,341 --> 00:40:45,260
Niagara Falls Power Company powerhouses number one

580
00:40:45,261 --> 00:40:50,500
and number two were permanently shut down on September 30, 1961,

581
00:40:50,501 --> 00:40:54,000
amid little fanfare.

582
00:40:54,001 --> 00:40:56,480
The Niagara Power Plants had moved easily

583
00:40:56,481 --> 00:40:59,280
into the second half of the 20th century,

584
00:40:59,281 --> 00:41:04,400
running smoothly, efficiently, all but invisible to the public.

585
00:41:04,401 --> 00:41:09,400
But all that changed on November 9, 1965.

586
00:41:09,500 --> 00:41:11,600
One moment, New York was glittering Gotham,

587
00:41:11,700 --> 00:41:13,140
the Great White Way ablaze.

588
00:41:13,240 --> 00:41:15,420
The next moment, only flickering candles,

589
00:41:15,520 --> 00:41:17,820
automobile headlights, and bobbing flashlights

590
00:41:17,920 --> 00:41:20,780
were probing a Stygian darkness.

591
00:41:20,880 --> 00:41:24,120
80,000 square miles of Northeastern North America

592
00:41:24,220 --> 00:41:27,560
were suddenly blacked out by the worst power failure

593
00:41:27,660 --> 00:41:30,420
in the history of the Western world.

594
00:41:30,520 --> 00:41:33,920
A small failsafe device in the Sir Adam Beck generating

595
00:41:33,921 --> 00:41:39,800
plant number two tripped and sent a power surge of current in the wrong direction.

596
00:41:39,801 --> 00:41:44,960
In just 12 minutes, 30 million people in eight states and the province of Ontario

597
00:41:44,961 --> 00:41:49,760
were without electricity. New York City would remain without electricity through

598
00:41:49,761 --> 00:41:56,240
the night. Finally, at dawn, service was restored and life went back to normal.

599
00:41:56,241 --> 00:42:01,700
The Niagara Power Project remains the largest electricity producer in New York

600
00:42:01,701 --> 00:42:06,040
state, meeting 10% of the state's overall power needs.

601
00:42:06,041 --> 00:42:11,700
But as its federally regulated license comes up for renewal in 2006, the Niagara Power

602
00:42:11,701 --> 00:42:16,940
Project must continue to reinvent itself and its ability to produce electricity.

603
00:42:16,941 --> 00:42:21,080
We're always looking at ways of improving our maintenance.

604
00:42:21,081 --> 00:42:26,400
We're looking at what technology can bring as far as improvements to what we have here.

605
00:42:26,401 --> 00:42:29,800
Many of the equipment we have, many of it's still original equipment.

606
00:42:29,801 --> 00:42:34,900
Much of it's been updated, but some of it is just the basic technologies of the conversion

607
00:42:34,901 --> 00:42:39,700
of hydropower into electricity hasn't changed much.

608
00:42:39,701 --> 00:42:46,300
So we're looking at all that and trying to keep pace with it and move forward.

609
00:42:46,301 --> 00:42:51,000
Renovation at the Moses plant won't be completed until 2006.

610
00:42:51,001 --> 00:42:56,920
It will upgrade the existing 13 turbine generators and will enable the plant to use 6 million

611
00:42:56,921 --> 00:43:02,680
extra gallons of water a minute, producing up to 14% more electricity during periods

612
00:43:02,681 --> 00:43:09,060
of high consumer demand. On the Canadian side, Ontario Hydro Company, the operators

613
00:43:09,061 --> 00:43:14,220
of the Sir Adam Beck plants, are also looking to expand.

614
00:43:14,221 --> 00:43:18,320
The use of the Niagara River is governed by an international treaty between Canada and

615
00:43:18,321 --> 00:43:19,267
the United States.

616
00:43:19,268 --> 00:43:23,000
On the Canadian side, we are not yet using our full potential available

617
00:43:23,001 --> 00:43:25,400
through the treaty.

618
00:43:25,401 --> 00:43:27,280
Right now, we are actively considering

619
00:43:27,281 --> 00:43:29,940
the construction of a new tunnel that

620
00:43:29,941 --> 00:43:33,380
would provide more water to the existing Beck power plants.

621
00:43:33,381 --> 00:43:36,020
The tunnel would be built below the existing tunnels

622
00:43:36,021 --> 00:43:39,780
and would be more than 12 miles long and 37 feet wide.

623
00:43:39,781 --> 00:43:44,680
The project is estimated to cost between $300 and $500 million.

624
00:43:44,681 --> 00:43:47,300
It would increase the power output of the Beck generating

625
00:43:47,301 --> 00:43:48,360
stations by 12%.

626
00:43:53,000 --> 00:44:00,380
For more than 100 years, Niagara Falls has served as inspiration for scientists and lovers of nature alike.

627
00:44:01,300 --> 00:44:04,080
Niagara Falls had always bespoken raw power.

628
00:44:05,160 --> 00:44:11,660
And for centuries, people had envisioned Niagara being put to use.

629
00:44:12,320 --> 00:44:19,540
And it was the Niagara Falls Power Company that truly did utilize the vast torrents of Niagara Falls.

630
00:44:23,000 --> 00:44:27,280
They managed to construct something that basically changed the world.

631
00:44:27,940 --> 00:44:30,620
It set the pace for our modern-day life.

632
00:44:31,320 --> 00:44:38,960
The American and Canadian Niagara power plants were the inspiration for today's massive electrical and hydroelectrical power ventures.

633
00:44:39,420 --> 00:44:45,880
For centuries, Niagara Falls has been a vision for dreamers mesmerized by the natural spectacle.

634
00:44:46,900 --> 00:44:52,380
Today, Niagara Falls power plants continue to delicately balance the fine line.

635
00:44:53,000 --> 00:44:55,640
between exploitation of the falls

636
00:44:55,641 --> 00:44:59,000
and our desire to use the energy that abides there.

